Android 15 / API 35: hook LocaleManager so a guest can read its own per-app locale.

SYMPTOM
    Chrome reaches its first-run screen inside a container and then dies on the main
    thread a few seconds later:

        FATAL EXCEPTION: main
        Process: com.android.chrome, PID: 10409
        java.lang.SecurityException: getApplicationLocales: Neither user 10234 nor
            current process has android.permission.READ_APP_SPECIFIC_LOCALES.
          at android.app.ILocaleManager$Stub$Proxy.getApplicationLocales
          at android.app.LocaleManager.getApplicationLocales(LocaleManager.java:126)
          at tj7.queueIdle

    10234 is the host (co.tdevs.duplika) UID. The call comes from an idle handler, so it
    lands after the UI is already up -- which is why Chrome appears to start and then
    vanishes, and why "Continue" often looks like the thing that broke it.

WHY IT HAPPENS
    LocaleManager.getApplicationLocales() invokes
    ILocaleManager.getApplicationLocales(packageName, userId). LocaleManagerService
    requires READ_APP_SPECIFIC_LOCALES unless the caller owns the package it is asking
    about. In a container the Binder calling UID is the host's while the package argument
    is the guest's, so the platform correctly sees a caller asking about a package it does
    not own.

WHY BCORE DID NOT CATCH IT
    Not a mis-registration this time -- there was no hook at all. Bcore has no
    ILocaleManagerProxy and never registered the "locale" service, so the call went to the
    real LocaleManagerService unchanged. Per-app locales are API 33+, newer than most of
    the proxy set.

THE FIX
    A new ILocaleManagerProxy, registered in HookManager beside IUserManagerProxy, hooking
    getApplicationLocales and setApplicationLocales. Both rewrite args[0] -- the package --
    to the host package, exactly as IUserManagerProxy's application-restrictions hook does.

    getWho() resolves android.app.ILocaleManager$Stub by reflection because
    black-reflection carries no stub for it, following the pattern
    ISensitiveContentProtectionManagerProxy already uses. inject() no-ops when the service
    is absent, so a platform below API 33 is unaffected.

SECURITY RATIONALE
    The same argument as patch 0006, and for the same reason it is not a bypass:

    - No UID, package, signature or certificate is spoofed, and no SecurityException is
      suppressed. The platform check still runs and passes on its own terms.
    - The platform rule is "the caller must own the package it asks about". Inside a
      container the caller genuinely IS the host, so asking about the host package is the
      truthful request rather than a forged one.
    - userId is untouched, so no other Android user is addressed.
    - The package argument is replaced with the host package, never an arbitrary one, so a
      guest cannot use this to read a third-party app's locale.
    - A virtual user is not an Android user and a guest has no separate per-app locale
      record at the platform level, so the host's value is the only legitimate answer. On a
      device where nobody has set a per-app override that value is empty, which is what the
      host itself receives.
    - Play Integrity, SafetyNet, GMS and account/OAuth paths are untouched. This does not
      affect the caller-identity boundary in docs/level10-gms-caller-identity-boundary.md;
      LocaleManager is a platform service, not a Google one.

    The setter is included deliberately. Fixing only the read path would move the crash one
    call later for any guest that sets its own locale.

VALIDATION
    Chrome in a container on the emulator of record, API 35: before this patch the
    SecurityException above is fatal and reproducible. Expected after: no
    READ_APP_SPECIFIC_LOCALES exception, and Chrome survives past the idle handler.

    Note what this patch does NOT claim to fix. The GoogleApiManager "Unknown calling
    package name" refusals in the same log are the documented caller-identity boundary and
    are unaffected -- they are non-fatal for Chrome. Renderer stability and page rendering
    (criteria 6 and 7 in docs/patch0008-...-verification.md) are separate and still open.

